<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Great Game</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Great Game</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Game was the nineteenth-century rivalry between the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empires</a> for influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Russian expansion towards Afghanistan brought the two empires close, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> served as a buffer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the Afghan frontier. It was eased by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> of 1907.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-great-game.jpg" alt="The Great Game" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Stanford Ltd. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanfords_Map_of_Western_Asia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> feared that Russian expansion to the south would threaten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> feared the spread of British interests into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. On one major view the rivalry began on 12 January 1830, when Lord Ellenborough, president of the Board of Control for India, told the governor-general, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-william-bentinck">Lord William Bentinck</a>, to open a trade route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Britain hoped to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and to use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> and Bukhara as buffer states. The term Great Game was coined in 1840 by the British intelligence officer Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly">Arthur Conolly</a> and was popularised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudyard-kipling">Rudyard Kipling</a>'s novel Kim of 1901.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> in 1843 and fought the two Anglo-Sikh Wars, in 1845–1846 and 1848–1849, which brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">North-West Frontier Province</a> under its rule. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> ended in 1856 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s defeat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II of Russia</a> waited some years and then expanded in two campaigns. Russia occupied Chimkent in 1864, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> in 1865, Khokhand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1866 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in 1868, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> in 1873.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was the main point of contact. In 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> sent a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> uninvited; when Sher Ali Khan refused a British mission and turned back its envoy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a> began, and 40,000 men invaded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in November 1878. The Treaty of Gandamak of 1879 and the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> left Afghanistan under British control of its foreign policy, with internal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, as a buffer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> was another field: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Persia fought in 1856–1857 after a Persian assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The two powers never fought each other directly in the Great Game, and they also cooperated. Their agreement of 21 January 1873 treated Badakhshan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakhan">Wakhan Corridor</a> as Afghan, and the Afghan Boundary Commission followed the protocol of 10 September 1885. A frontier defined by the Pamir Boundary Commission in 1895 is the date many historians give for the end of the game. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> of August 1907 placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the British sphere and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and northern Iran in the Russian sphere, and treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> as neutral.</p><p>Historians do not agree on the dating. Edward Ingram places the beginning in 1832–1834 and the end in 1842, with the British withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, while Konstantin Penzev dates its start to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. David X. Noack sees a later contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1919 to 1933.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Game” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qajar Iran became an arena of rivalry between Britain and Russia in the <b>Great Game</b>, and concessions to foreign companies, such as the Reuter concession of 1872 and the oil concession granted to William Knox D’Arcy in 1901, provoked lasting resentment of foreign influence; the D’Arcy concession led…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The subjugation of the Central Asian khanates of Bukhara (1868), Khiva (1873) and Kokand (1876) brought the frontier to the borders of Afghanistan and British India, and generated the '<b>Great Game</b>' rivalry with Britain that governed Central Asian political geography until the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sikh Empire was a buffer between British India and the unsettled Afghan frontier at a critical stage of the <b>Great Game</b>, the rivalry between the British and Russian Empires for influence in Central and South Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Herat was a walled city on the route between Persia, Central Asia and India, and its strategic value made it central to the <b>Great Game</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-herat">Principality of Herat</a></li></ul></section>
